    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3, Chap 618 of 1998",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7816\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend chapter 618 of the laws of 1998, amending the general municipal\nlaw and the education law relating to disposal of surplus computer\nequipment by political subdivisions, in relation to the effectiveness\nthereof\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThis legislation would extend existing law that enables local\npolitical subdivision to donate excess computer equipment to\nlibraries, schools or nonprofit educational programs.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill would amend section 3 of chapter 618 of the laws of 1998 as\nlast amended by chapter 158 of the laws of 2007 to extend the\neffective date until July 1, 2013.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nComputer equipment that has become out-dated or in excess for the\npurposes of a political subdivision may still have value for a\nlibrary, school, or other secular educational program, and this law\nallows such donations. The law requires a competitive request for\nproposals process and prohibits the transfer of software if doing so\nmight violate licensing agreements or copyright laws. This law has\nbeen extended repeatedly.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill; however, this provision has previously been\nextended in:\n\n2007: Chapter 158\n2004: Chapter 130\n2001: Chapter 98\n\nIt originally became law in 1998 as Chapter 618.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill is not expected to have any cost to the state.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
